From 522e5b454de677db2bbbb599f5adb5d1fc866403 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 16:49:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] kanban comment writer: automation runners, systemd
 services and agent handles record agent

Three hub-chat agent notes posted through hub-worker-exec (SSM SendCommand) recorded
writer_kind=human (comments 41678, 562861, 16922), as did 14 systemd-timer automation comments
and 15 cron job-state comments. A `human` note is read as David's words.

Five whys:
1. The CLI writer records human only after a complete ancestor walk to init finds no marker.
2. Under SSM the comment process descends bash <- runuser <- ssm-document-worker <-
   ssm-agent-worker <- amazon-ssm-agent <- init; the root-owned ancestors are skipped as
   foreign, and the script's environment is the agent's plus HOME/USER/PATH: no markers.
3. Every relay had to forward the caller's markers itself (ssh AcceptEnv, the courier fix);
   hub-worker-exec is the third relay and forwarded none.
Root: provenance depended on each relay; the writer had no rule for commands started by a
non-interactive automation runner, so every new relay (and every timer/cron producer)
defaulted to human, the direction that grants more authority. Not caught: each earlier fix
tested only its own relay; no test modelled a tree rooted at a root-owned runner.

Fix (hermes_cli/kanban_comment_writer.py). Each rule only ever lowers a note's authority:
- Runner rule: an ancestor named amazon-ssm-agent, ssm-document-worker, cron, crond or atd
  (also the 15-char comm prefix) ends the walk as agent, `<pid>:runner:<name>`, even when
  root-owned. An interactive ssm-session-worker below it disables the rule, so
  `aws ssm start-session` shells stay human. Names are read only with the real reader: a
  substituted ProcReader models its own pids, and real names for those could be anything.
- systemd rule: a clean walk from a process whose cgroup v2 leaf is a `.service` under
  /system.slice/ or /user.slice/.../user@<uid>.service/ records agent, `runner:systemd:<unit>`.
  session-*.scope (ssh/login), other .scope leaves, delegated subtrees and v1-only hosts keep
  the walk. The real /proc/self/cgroup is read only with the real reader.
- Handle rule: an --author of the form `<gateway>/t_<16 hex>` or `hub-chat-<6+ hex>` turns a
  clean walk into agent (`author_handle`) and logs
  `comment_writer: author handle overrides human walk`.
Unchanged: an agent walk keeps its own evidence; the typed `/kanban` slash path still passes
human explicitly; an `env -i` comment from a human login shell is still human. ProcReader and
agent_ancestor keep their contracts (the HTTP writer and existing fakes are untouched).
Out of scope: hub-worker-exec marker forwarding (state repo, t_01a1d577aa7c07d5); macOS
launchd (t_01a1d59a6e097257).

Proof:
- RED: tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_cli_comment_writer.py with production reverted:
  runner+handle slice 28 failed / 42 passed (the SSM end-to-end comment recorded
  'human'); cgroup slice 22 failed / 72 passed. GREEN: 95 passed.
- 8 provenance suites (cli/http writers, user manager, dashboard actor, real client, writer
  kind, open-url): 173 passed, 1 skipped.
- Service-runner simulation (`_self_cgroup` forced to a system.slice .service, as on a
  systemd CI runner) over all 57 test files that mention writer_kind/kanban comment/run_slash:
  2043 passed, 0 failed, so no existing human expectation depends on the real cgroup.
- Mutations (sdlc-review mutants.py, 24 mutants of every new condition): 23 killed. The one
  survivor, `fullmatch(str(author))`, is equivalent: "None" never matches a handle.
